! vfl_crossing_store.strap -- a VFL STORE whose field straddles two
! storage words.
!
! WHY IT EXISTS, AND WHO ASKED FOR IT.  MCP did.  Printed p.43 line 49
! of IBM's own operating system is
!
!       Q ST    ST(V+I)(BU,6,6), .6($5)
!
! the store at the bottom of the Hollerith-to-BCD conversion loop that
! printed p.38 lines 11-15 call to read an IOD control card.  It stores
! six bits at a time and advances six bits at a time, and SIXTY-FOUR IS
! NOT A MULTIPLE OF SIX: character ten of every card lands at bits
! 60-65 and straddles the word boundary.
!
! Until 2026-10-24 the model refused that store -- StretchExecute's ST
! arm called setOpInvalid on any crossing field, which bsv/README.md's
! scope list recorded.  The refusal cost more than the one character.
! A suppressed VFL operation also skips its progressive index advance
! (Reference Manual printed p.21), so the output pointer STAYED at bit
! 60 and the remaining seventy characters were refused at the same
! place: MCP's card converter produced TEN characters out of eighty,
! "IOD" arrived as "I", the card type did not match Y IOD MK, and MCP
! rejected its own configuration card.  The measurement is in
! tests/gen_mcp_boot_tb.py (mcp_ucbf1_0, mcp_ucbf1_1, mcp_ucbf2_0).
!
! THE SOURCE THAT SAYS A CROSSING STORE IS LEGAL is that line.  Rule 4
! asks for a primary source and MCP's listing is one: the operating
! system the machine shipped with depends on it, so the refusal was
! never a documented restriction -- it was a one-write-per-rule
! toolchain limit, recorded as such in StretchExecute.bsv's header in
! the same breath as "Transmit moves only one word", which was the same
! limit and was lifted the same way.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD, STATED PLAINLY.  MCP's rows pin line 49's ENCODING
! bit-exactly (decks/mcp_bootstrap_p43.strap) and the boot run pins its
! EFFECT on one card.  This program is hand-written from Reference
! Manual printed p.21 (the VFL field at a bit address) and printed p.31
! (the straddling rule for address monitoring), and its expectations
! were computed in Python from that arithmetic BEFORE the arm was
! built.  It is weaker than a deck oracle and stronger than the boot
! run alone, because it varies the width and the offset.
!
! EVERY TARGET STARTS AS ALL ONES, which is not decoration: a store
! that wrote a whole word instead of a field, or that masked the wrong
! half, leaves ones where the derivation says zeros and the other way
! round.  A zeroed target would hide both.
!
! HOW THE EXPECTATIONS WERE DERIVED.  A field at bit address A, length
! L, occupies IBM bits A..A+L-1 of the two-word pair; so for the pair
! (W, W+1) seen as one 128-bit quantity the field is at 128-A-L from
! the bottom, and the halves come back out at the word boundary:
!
!   32 bits at .48, value 0x12345678  -> W   bits 48-63 = 0x1234
!                                        W+1 bits  0-15 = 0x5678
!    6 bits at .60, value (8)46       -> W   bits 60-63 = 1001
!                                        W+1 bits  0-1  = 10
!
! BLOCK 4 IS THE ONE THAT REPRODUCES THE MCP BUG.  Two progressive
! stores through one index: the first crosses, the second must land six
! bits further on.  If the advance is lost -- which is exactly what the
! refusal did -- the second store repeats the first and T E+1 reads
! (16)BFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF instead of (16)A6FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF's pattern.
! The two expectations differ, so this block cannot pass either way.
!
! BLOCK 2 IS THE ONE THAT ATTACKS THE STALL.  StretchStorage's
! writeWordTail parks the second word for one clock and StretchCore
! holds off arming while it waits (storeTailPending, folded into
! busyXfer).  Two crossing stores BACK TO BACK put the first store's
! tail write and the second store's head write one clock apart, which
! is the collision that stall exists to prevent -- and if it is ever
! reached without it, StretchStorage says so out loud ("arrived while a
! crossing-field tail was pending and was LOST").
!
! >>> AND THE STALL SURVIVED ITS INJECTION ON THE BOOT RUN. <<<
! Removing `st.storeTailPending()` from busyXfer left
! tests/gen_mcp_boot_tb.py entirely green -- MCP's loop never puts two
! stores close enough together -- so the guard is argued from the
! mechanism and not yet from a measurement.  Recorded here rather than
! left to be assumed; block 2 is the attempt to make it load-bearing.
!
! WHAT IS STILL REFUSED, and block 5 is the scope check for it:
!   * M+ with a crossing field, and the CM connective likewise.
!     Nothing measured needs them; bsv/README.md carries them with the
!     date.
!   * A crossing field whose SECOND word is below 32 (the machine's
!     registers): the tail is drained to the core-storage queue and
!     does not run writeWord's special-address dispatch.
!   * A crossing field that ALSO needs a progressive refill: that
!     wants three reads of a two-slot prefetch.

        SLC, (8)300.0
START   LVI, $X1, 0

! ---- 1. a 32-bit field at .48: sixteen bits either side -----------
        L(BU,32,8), DATA1
        ST(BU,32,8), TA+.48

! ---- 2. BACK TO BACK, the stall's own case ------------------------
! Two crossing stores with nothing between them.  Separate targets, so
! neither can be mistaken for the other's work.
        L(BU,32,8), DATA1
        ST(BU,32,8), TB+.48
        ST(BU,32,8), TC+.48

! ---- 3. MCP's width and MCP's offset: six bits at .60 -------------
        L(BU,6,6), DATA2
        ST(BU,6,6), TD+.60

! ---- 4. TWO PROGRESSIVE STORES, THE FIRST OF THEM CROSSING -------
! This is printed p.43 line 49's own shape.  The second store proves
! the index advanced PAST the crossing field; if it did not, it
! overwrites the first and the check below fails with the derivation's
! other value.
! >>> AND THE INDEX IS LOADED FROM A DATA WORD, NOT BY LVI, WHICH IS
! >>> A CORRECTION THIS PROGRAM EARNED THE HARD WAY. <<<
!
! The first draft wrote "LVI,$X2,TE+.60" and the two stores landed at
! bits 32-37 and 38-43 instead of 60-65 and 66-71.  LVI's ADDRESS
! FIELD IS NINETEEN BITS -- eighteen of word and ONE of half word --
! so a bit offset of 60 cannot be expressed in it and 60 became 32.
!
! THE PRINTED LISTING IS WHAT SAYS SO, and it says it twice on one
! page.  MCP printed p.43 line 33 is "LVI,$3,5.0" and compiles as
! 000005.07 01; line 38 is "LVI,$3,5.32" and compiles as
! 000005.47 01.  The ONLY difference is bit 18, the half-word
! selector: 0o07 = 000111 against 0o47 = 100111, with $3 in bits
! 19-22 and the flag in bit 23 unchanged.  Both rows are checked
! bit-exactly by tests/verify_mcp_bootstrap_p43.py.
!
! AND IBM NEVER ASKS FOR A FINER ONE.  Every LVI in MCP's converter
! uses .0 or .32 -- lines 33, 38, 42, 44 -- because that is all the
! field holds.  A full 24-bit bit address has to come from a DATA
! WORD, which is exactly why printed p.38 line 15's call block is
! "VF,Y UCBF1" and printed p.43 line 15 reads it with
! "LV,$5,1.0($15)".  This block uses that idiom because it is the one
! the operation requires, not merely the one MCP happens to use.
!
! THE ASSEMBLER ROUNDED SILENTLY and nothing here says whether real
! STRAP diagnosed it; no source this project has read speaks to it, so
! no diagnostic was added on a guess.  docs/NEXT.md carries it.
        LV, $X2, TEADDR
        L(BU,6,6), DATA2
        ST(V+I)(BU,6,6), .6($X2)
        ST(V+I)(BU,6,6), .6($X2)

! ---- nothing so far may have been refused ------------------------
        BOP, $                       ! hang if OP came on: all legal

! ---- 5. M+ ACROSS A BOUNDARY IS STILL REFUSED --------------------
! The scope check, and it is LAST because OP is sticky: every block
! above has already been tested for a clear OP.
        M+(BU,32,8), TF+.48
        BZOP, $                      ! hang if OP did NOT come on
        B, PASS
PASS    BEW, $
        CNOP

! ---- data --------------------------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)400.0
DATA1   DD(BU,32), (16)12345678
DATA2   DD(BU,6), (8)46
! The bit address block 4's index is loaded from.  A VF compiles 25
! bits and the location counter stands at bit 25 afterwards (C22-6531
! printed p.18), so it gets a word of its own.
        SLC, (8)401.0
TEADDR  VF, TE+.60
! Every target is TWO words of ones, because a crossing store changes
! both and the second is where the dropped-tail bug lives.
        SLC, (8)410.0
TA      DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        SLC, (8)420.0
TB      DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        SLC, (8)430.0
TC      DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        SLC, (8)440.0
TD      DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        SLC, (8)450.0
TE      DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        SLC, (8)460.0
TF      DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        DD(BU,64), (8)1777777777777777777777
        END, START
